WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.917
SPEAKER: How are you
coordinating and managing

00:00:01.917 --> 00:00:02.940
your workflows?

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.670
If you're using cron
jobs with custom scripts,

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.160
you know that it can get super
tricky to stitch together

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.810
all of your services
and applications

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.690
together to build a workflow,
especially as your workflow

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.090
scales in size and complexity.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.910
In this video, we'll explore
some of the orchestration

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.280
services that Google Cloud
has to support your workflows.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.460
So what is orchestration?

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.340
Orchestration refers to the
automated configuration,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.630
coordination, and management of
computer systems and services.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.970
You can think of
orchestration as the process

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.190
of stitching all of your
services and applications

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:38.010
together to build an
end-to-end workflow.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.130
Perhaps you want to automate the
execution of a single service

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.290
at a specific time of the day.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.720
Or maybe you have a more
sophisticated approach

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.910
of not just
automating one service

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.670
but automating,
coordinating, and monitoring

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.868
multiple services over
longer periods of time.

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:55.410
There are several
orchestration tools

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.200
that Google Cloud has to
help support your workflows.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.810
These include Cloud
Scheduler for scheduling

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.510
single repetitive
tasks, Workflows

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.850
for complex multi-service
orchestration,

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:09.440
and Cloud Composer for
data-driven workloads.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.780
First, we have Cloud Scheduler.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.300
Cloud Scheduler is a
simple scheduling tool

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.190
that allows you to
schedule the execution

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:19.070
of a single HTTP-based
service on a recurring

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.080
schedule you define.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.230
Many customers use Scheduler
alongside other Google Cloud

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:26.600
tools like Pub/Sub
and Cloud Functions

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.660
to execute their code
in a serverless manner.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.340
Cloud Scheduler is
great if you need

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.430
to call a single service
at regular intervals.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.620
For example, you could
use Cloud Scheduler

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.050
to schedule the execution
of a Cloud Function

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.240
that sends out daily
emails with top news

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.960
stories about technology.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.760
But what if you have
multiple services

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.370
that you need to chain together,
feeding the output of one

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.170
service to the next?

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.500
Or what if you need to apply
complex logic to determine how

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.810
and when services are invoked?

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.270
This brings us to Workflows.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.400
Workflows is a service
for orchestrating

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.120
multiple HTTP-based services
into a durable and stateful

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.610
workflow.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.860
Like Cloud Scheduler,
Workflows allows

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.370
you to automate the execution
of HTTP-based services running

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.120
on Cloud Functions and Cloud
Run as well as external services

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.970
and APIs.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.430
But unlike Scheduler,
Workflows gives you

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:22.190
the power to connect multiple
microservices as part

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.080
of your larger workflow.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.820
When you create
your workflow, you

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.100
can use either YAML or JSON.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.260
You specify the
order of the services

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.300
as well as how the
result of one step

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.820
can be used as either an
input to the next step

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.950
or as a condition to determine
which step to execute next.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:40.700
Workflows is perfect
for chaining together

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.520
microservices, automating
infrastructure tasks

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.940
like starting or stopping a
VM, and integrating reliably

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.830
with external systems.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.530
To execute a workflow, you
can manually trigger it

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:54.230
via an API call or
in the Cloud Console,

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:57.920
or you can set up a recurring
schedule with Cloud Scheduler.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.160
If your focus is on engineering
data pipelines or big data

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:04.460
processing, you should
consider Cloud Composer.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.710
Composer is a service
designed to orchestrate

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:09.680
data-driven workflows and
is built on the popular open

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.350
source Apache Airflow project.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.000
Since it's a fully
managed service,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.730
you don't have to worry about
managing Airflow deployments.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.040
Composer takes care of
infrastructure management

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.860
for you so you can focus on
actually building your data

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:23.270
pipelines.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.770
It also supports your
pipelines wherever they are,

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:28.040
whether that be on premise
or across multiple Cloud

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.760
platforms.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.890
Like Workflows,
you create a task

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.410
for each step in your
workflow, configure the order

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.500
of the tasks, and specify
which tasks to execute next

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.540
based on some conditions.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.590
Tasks are expressed as Python
Directed Acyclic Graph, also

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.930
known as a DAG, that
can be scheduled

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.240
to run at a time of your
choice or in response

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.800
to a particular event.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.510
Operators allow
you to communicate

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.930
with services across multiple
cloud environments and on-prem.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.660
In addition to many operators
in the Apache Airflow ecosystem,

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:02.280
there are over 150 operators
for Google Cloud alone.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.290
For example, your
Composer workflow

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.800
might start by triggering a
data ingestion job in BigQuery,

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.630
then start a batch
processing dataflow pipeline,

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:13.320
and finally send a Slack
notification to teammates

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.410
that the data processing
workload has finished.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:19.529
Both Composer and Workflows
are great for orchestrating

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:23.470
multiple services and handling
long-running workflows.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.060
However, there are several
key differences to highlight.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.160
Composer is designed
for orchestration

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:32.730
of the transformation of data as
part of ETL or data engineering

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.490
workflows.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.590
Workflows, on the
other hand, is focus on

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.310
the orchestration of
HTTP-based services built

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:44.720
with Cloud Functions, Cloud
Run, and external APIs.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.790
While Composer is
designed for orchestrating

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:49.460
batch workloads that can
handle a few seconds of delay

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.310
between tasks,
Workflows is designed

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.100
for more latency-sensitive
use cases.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.020
And while Composer
tasks can be modeled

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:00.400
as a directed acyclic
graph, Workflows

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.920
can work for use cases where
you need loops and jumps

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.630
to already executed steps.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:08.260
So there you have it-- an
introduction to orchestration

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:11.620
in Google Cloud and how you
can use Scheduler, Composer,

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:15.430
and Workflows to manage and
configure your services.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.620
Check out the next video
on data orchestration

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:19.560
with Cloud Composer.

